---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The six modules the ship carries, in the order a build passes them. This is the text equivalent
// of the scene's aria-hidden labels (src/scene/local/ship.ts): the canvas cannot be read, so the
// same names have to exist in the prose. Keep the two lists together.
const stages = [
  { name: 'Build', note: 'Compile and unit test' },
  { name: 'Scan', note: 'Image and policy gate' },
  { name: 'Publish', note: 'Scanned image to the lab registry' },
  { name: 'Deploy', note: 'Reconciled onto the cluster' },
  { name: 'Control', note: 'Where a release is approved' },
  { name: 'Promote', note: 'Progressive delivery to production' },
];
---
<Base title="GitOps" description="How a push to main builds this site in a Kubernetes cluster and reaches production through a pull request and a blue/green rollout.">
  <h1>How this site reaches production</h1>
  <p class="dek">A push to main builds this site inside the cluster, and a pull request is the only way it gets to production.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The cluster's state lives in a Git repository, and Flux reconciles the cluster to match it. My own applications are plain manifests. Third-party software such as Traefik and the monitoring stack comes in as Helm charts pinned to exact versions through Flux HelmReleases, and application secrets come from Vault through External Secrets Operator. The CNI is the one exception. Flux runs on the pod network, so it can't install the plugin it depends on, and I install Cilium by hand from values kept in the same repository.</p>
    <p>Every push to this site's repository starts a Jenkins pipeline that runs in pods on the cluster. Kaniko builds the image without a Docker daemon, and the site's own build runs inside it with two gates: the 3D scene has to stay under 250 KB gzipped, and a disclosure scan fails the build if a published page contains an address, a version or a VLAN ID. Unit tests for both gates run first, feeding each one pages built to fail it, so a gate that silently stops catching anything fails the build too. Kaniko writes the image to a file rather than a registry, and Trivy scans that file, failing the pipeline on any critical vulnerability. Trivy also checks the Dockerfile against its misconfiguration policies, such as a container left running as root, and fails on anything rated high or critical. Only a scanned image from <code>main</code> is pushed to the private registry in the lab, so an image that fails the scan never reaches it. On any other branch the pipeline stops after the scan.</p>
    <h2>Staging, then a pull request</h2>
    <p>Jenkins doesn't deploy anything. It updates the image tag in the staging manifest, runs the same misconfiguration policies against the staging manifests, and only then commits the change to the Flux repository, which rolls it out. Flagger runs a blue/green analysis on the new version before it takes traffic. It checks the health endpoint and that the home page carries my name, then requests each of the five pages every 30 seconds for four rounds. Two failed checks roll it back.</p>
    <p>Production is a separate namespace. The only way in is a pull request that copies the tag that passed staging into the production manifest. I review and merge it, Flux applies it, and Flagger runs the same blue/green analysis again in production before the new version takes any traffic there.</p>
  </div>
  <section>
    <h2>Stages</h2>
    <ul class="stages">
      {stages.map((stage) => <li>{stage.name} <span>{stage.note}</span></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
